<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
    HealthLogWidgets (app-extension) Privacy Manifest.

    Apple's privacy-manifest rule is per-binary; the widget extension is a
    separate binary from the host app, and ITMS-91053 static analysis scans the
    appex's linked symbols independently.

    Rationale (audit v0.16.2 H3 — the earlier "no Required-Reason API" claim was
    stale once the shared Services set grew):
    - The widget appex compiles `Services/AIConsentStore.swift`
      (`private let defaults: UserDefaults`, `.standard` read/write) and
      `Services/FeatureFlagsService.swift` (`UserDefaultsFeatureFlagsService`)
      from the project.yml HealthLogWidgets source set, so the binary links the
      UserDefaults Required-Reason API → NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults
      / CA92.1 is declared (same reason as the host app manifest). Leaving it
      empty is the under-declaration / rejection-risk direction.
    - The widget's own persistence (`WidgetSnapshotStore`) uses only the
      App-Group snapshot file via `Data(contentsOf:)` / atomic write. But the
      appex source set is wider than that store: it also compiles
      `Services/DoctorReportTmpSweeper.swift` (project.yml), which reads
      `.contentModificationDateKey` / `contentModificationDate` at :92,104,105 to
      decide whether a stale temp export may be deleted. That links the
      FileTimestamp Required-Reason API into THIS binary, so the category is
      declared below (audit v1.4.1 row 19 — the earlier "grep-verified: no
      contentModificationDate call sites" claim went stale when the sweeper
      entered the widget source closure; under-declaring is what triggers the
      ITMS-91053 mail after upload).
    - Still no SystemBootTime: re-grepped the full 395-file appex source closure
      for `systemUptime` / `mach_absolute_time` / `creationDate`
      and found none. `DispatchTime.now().uptimeNanoseconds` in
      `Cache/SWRSignpost.swift` is a libdispatch monotonic clock, not one of the
      listed boot-time symbols, and the host-app manifest does not declare the
      category for it either.
    - The extension does NO independent data collection or transmission. It only
      renders mood/medication data the host app already wrote into the shared
      App-Group container; the host app's own `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` declares
      the underlying collection (HealthFitness etc.). The extension's interactive
      quick-log buttons fire shared App Intents that reuse the host's server-first
      write path — no parallel collection path lives in this binary →
      NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is empty.
    - No tracking, no third-party SDKs, no tracking domains.
    -->
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>
    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>
        <!--
        UserDefaults — the appex links UserDefaults via the compiled
        AIConsentStore (BYO-consent flags) + UserDefaultsFeatureFlagsService.
        Reason CA92.1: "Access info about the user from the same app, with no
        need for it to be available across the network or to other apps".
        Mirrors the host-app manifest's UserDefaults declaration.
        -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>CA92.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <!--
        FileTimestamp — the appex links the API through the compiled
        `DoctorReportTmpSweeper`, which reads a temp export's modification date
        inside the app's own container before deleting it. DDA9.1 ("access file
        timestamps of files inside the app container") is that call site's exact
        reason; C617.1 mirrors the host-app manifest, which declares the same
        pair for the shared Services set. Same category, same file paths, two
        binaries.
        -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>C617.1</string>
                <string>DDA9.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
